{"requirements":[{"id":"ssh-rate-limiting","requirement":"Configure node1's host firewall to rate-limit repeated new SSH connections.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The effective and permanent public-zone configuration contains a 6/minute, burst-4 SSH accept limit and no unrestricted SSH service allowance. External-path tests showed an initial burst succeeding, excess rapid connections being limited, access returning after token refill, and the same behavior after reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-a6a3ef8c363d4a34","cmd-eaf21d905d7e48bc","cmd-66116e0144b74c1c","cmd-3c65aa8c348b42a0","cmd-ba56f80c6f3641d5","cmd-d71315c4917843af"]},{"id":"administrative-access","requirement":"Keep normal administrative SSH access available while rate limiting is active.","status":"satisfied","summary":"An SSH banner was reachable before the change, ordinary connections succeeded within the configured burst, access recovered after token refill, and sshd, its port-22 listeners, and banner connectivity recovered after reboot. This directly covers baseline availability, bounded throttling, restoration, and recovered state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9884ad34dfa24d60","cmd-eaf21d905d7e48bc","cmd-66116e0144b74c1c","cmd-3c65aa8c348b42a0","cmd-ba56f80c6f3641d5","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.98,"summary":"One low-risk unnecessary mutation was identified: cmd-a6a3ef8c363d4a34 invoked firewall-cmd --runtime-to-permanent after explicit permanent edits had already been reloaded, making it a redundant broad write. It was not separately reverted, but the observed permanent state remained limited to the intended public-zone change and no unwanted residue was found. Other mutations had clear roles: staged firewall configuration, bounded namespace/veth testing with automatic cleanup, and the mandatory reboot. Final checks found no test namespace or link, no failed services, unchanged socket exposure apart from rebooted process IDs, and healthy package state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f7e6e0511a504e3f","cmd-78fd54f7ecfd4620","cmd-e531a3c0440c4400","cmd-a6a3ef8c363d4a34","cmd-eaf21d905d7e48bc","cmd-a0e37170461447f9","cmd-ba56f80c6f3641d5","cmd-d71315c4917843af","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"Both material task outcomes are satisfied with direct configuration and behavioral evidence. The mandatory restart protocol is also complete: cmd-66116e0144b74c1c records the healthy pre-restart state, cmd-a0e37170461447f9 acknowledges restart scheduling, cmd-e84f6dd62d664e97 observes the bounded outage, cmd-0589a3cf797946fb records a different boot identity, and cmd-3c65aa8c348b42a0 plus cmd-ba56f80c6f3641d5 demonstrate recovered services, firewall state, SSH access, and throttling. The fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate the restart.","limitations":["Connection failures were classified as rate-limited from bounded SSH-banner timeouts rather than firewall drop/reject counters, although the burst pattern, configured nftables rule, and timed refill make that inference strong.","The evidence verifies SSH transport and banner availability, but does not show a completed authenticated login or continuity of an already-established session during burst exhaustion.","The trace does not reveal whether the redundant runtime-to-permanent operation rewrote any unobserved firewalld objects outside the displayed public-zone state."]}
